WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:01.616
- Four and a half years ago,

00:00:01.616 --> 00:00:03.754
my husband was misdiagnosed with cancer

00:00:03.754 --> 00:00:05.849
and this is that story.

00:00:05.849 --> 00:00:07.134
Picture this.

00:00:07.134 --> 00:00:08.375
2012.

00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:09.457
Things are amazing.

00:00:09.457 --> 00:00:11.683
I had been dating Jake
for about six months

00:00:11.683 --> 00:00:13.722
and we were obsessed with each other,

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:15.170
as young couples are.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.582
So we are moving in together

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:17.537
and it was really exciting.

00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:20.867
Jake's mom, after a 10
year battle with cancer,

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.616
had just been given a
clean bill of health.

00:00:22.616 --> 00:00:23.879
So we were all feeling pretty good.

00:00:23.879 --> 00:00:26.458
After maybe a week of living together,

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:28.719
Jake woke up one morning and was like,

00:00:28.719 --> 00:00:29.945
"My neck hurts."

00:00:29.945 --> 00:00:32.066
I was lying down and
couldn't really see him

00:00:32.066 --> 00:00:34.979
so I was like, "You probably
just slept on it funny."

00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:36.716
And he was like, "No.

00:00:36.716 --> 00:00:38.552
"It feels really weird."

00:00:38.552 --> 00:00:40.043
When I did look up,

00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:42.118
it looked like Jake had a literal baseball

00:00:42.118 --> 00:00:43.514
inside his neck.

00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:45.519
Obviously we were both
a little freaked out.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:47.978
I called my dad right away
because he's a doctor.

00:00:47.978 --> 00:00:50.146
He's a gynecologist, but whatever.

00:00:50.146 --> 00:00:50.979
It's all the same.

00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:52.665
I told him Jake's symptoms and he said

00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:54.340
"It sounds like it could be mono."

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.945
So I was like, "Okay, maybe that's it."

00:00:55.945 --> 00:00:57.763
Jake wasn't in too much pain so we decided

00:00:57.763 --> 00:00:59.560
to wait a day before seeing a doctor.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.075
The next day, his neck had
only increased in size.

00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:04.695
Instead of seeing a specialist,

00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:07.211
he decided to see whoever
had the soonest appointment.

00:01:07.211 --> 00:01:09.544
The doctor was like, "What the fuck?

00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:10.898
"I have no idea.

00:01:10.898 --> 00:01:13.329
"You need to go to this ear,
nose, and throat specialist."

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:16.496
So we went and this frazzled
little doctor comes in

00:01:16.496 --> 00:01:21.101
with these magnifying glasses
on his head and he's like,

00:01:21.101 --> 00:01:24.851
"We have reason to
believe this is lymphoma."

00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:29.454
Now keep in mind, Jake's mom had literally

00:01:29.454 --> 00:01:33.164
just recovered from the same
type of cancer a week ago.

00:01:33.164 --> 00:01:35.414
So I was just like, "Nope."

00:01:36.528 --> 00:01:38.891
I asked the doctor if they
were gonna check for mono

00:01:38.891 --> 00:01:40.942
and he was like, "There's no way.

00:01:40.942 --> 00:01:41.907
"There's no need.

00:01:41.907 --> 00:01:44.252
"It can't be mono 'cause
his neck is too big.

00:01:44.252 --> 00:01:45.836
"It's 100% lymphoma."

00:01:45.836 --> 00:01:48.431
So they did a needle
biopsy right then and there

00:01:48.431 --> 00:01:49.968
and ran it through a lab while we sat

00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:51.127
in the waiting room.

00:01:51.127 --> 00:01:53.636
They finally called us in and they said,

00:01:53.636 --> 00:01:55.355
"The mass is monoclonal,

00:01:55.355 --> 00:01:56.690
"so it's most likely lymphoma,

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.036
"we just don't know what type."

00:01:58.036 --> 00:02:00.069
So they referred us to
an oncologist at a big

00:02:00.069 --> 00:02:01.445
New York City hospital.

00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:04.034
Coincidentally, the
same hospital Jake's mom

00:02:04.034 --> 00:02:05.773
had just been discharged from.

00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:07.956
The oncologist we met
with agreed that it was

00:02:07.956 --> 00:02:09.575
definitely presenting as lymphoma.

00:02:09.575 --> 00:02:12.121
I asked again, had they checked for mono?

00:02:12.121 --> 00:02:12.983
I was ignored.

00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:15.453
They might have well said, "Lauren, shhh.

00:02:15.453 --> 00:02:16.541
"The men are talking."

00:02:16.541 --> 00:02:18.709
I was getting increasingly frustrated.

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:20.556
The oncologist sent us to a surgeon to get

00:02:20.556 --> 00:02:21.676
a bigger biopsy.

00:02:21.676 --> 00:02:24.038
The oncologist and the surgeon both agreed

00:02:24.038 --> 00:02:26.131
at the rate Jake's neck was expanding,

00:02:26.131 --> 00:02:28.300
it was an aggressive cancer and we needed

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.570
to get a sample right away.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:30.924
Since they were in such a rush

00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:32.979
and there wasn't any room in the OR,

00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:34.754
they decide to do the biopsy right there

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:35.830
in the exam room.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.813
The next day we came in and we expected it

00:02:37.813 --> 00:02:42.028
to just be like a simple
snip of Jake's neck.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.068
It wasn't.

00:02:43.068 --> 00:02:45.126
So the surgeon opened Jake's neck

00:02:45.126 --> 00:02:47.404
while I sat there in the
room for moral support.

00:02:47.404 --> 00:02:49.316
But as he tried to get some tissue,

00:02:49.316 --> 00:02:51.006
something went really wrong.

00:02:51.006 --> 00:02:53.950
It was harder to get tissue
from the mass than expected

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.104
and Jake was starting to bleed a lot.

00:02:56.104 --> 00:02:59.655
For about 30 minutes the
nurse held Jake's neck open

00:02:59.655 --> 00:03:02.978
with these clamps while
the doctor prodded around.

00:03:02.978 --> 00:03:04.348
He couldn't get anything,

00:03:04.348 --> 00:03:07.148
and at this point, we were
very deep into Jake's neck.

00:03:07.148 --> 00:03:09.995
So Jake was only under local anesthetic

00:03:09.995 --> 00:03:12.125
and it was starting to wear off.

00:03:12.125 --> 00:03:14.608
Now, Jake could feel everything.

00:03:14.608 --> 00:03:17.196
The bleeding started
to get so out of hand,

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.984
he needed to address that
instead of getting any tissue.

00:03:19.984 --> 00:03:21.995
Meanwhile, I was holding Jake's hand,

00:03:21.995 --> 00:03:24.034
who was gripping it for dear life.

00:03:24.034 --> 00:03:26.937
At one point, the doctor
even had me turning knobs

00:03:26.937 --> 00:03:28.604
on the cauterization machine 'cause

00:03:28.604 --> 00:03:29.932
there weren't enough nurses.

00:03:29.932 --> 00:03:31.963
And Jake's legs were twitching every time

00:03:31.963 --> 00:03:33.747
they poked him with the cauterizer.

00:03:33.747 --> 00:03:35.682
They finally closed Jake's
neck but they didn't

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.584
get enough of a sample
as they needed so it

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:38.824
was basically for nothing.

00:03:38.824 --> 00:03:41.021
We scheduled a biopsy for the next day

00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:43.530
in an actual operating room.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.869
Luckily, this biopsy went smoothly.

00:03:45.869 --> 00:03:47.453
Jake got to sleep through this one,

00:03:47.453 --> 00:03:49.065
and shout out to Candy
Crush for getting me

00:03:49.065 --> 00:03:50.075
through all the waiting.

00:03:50.075 --> 00:03:51.790
In the meantime,

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.211
since this was such an aggressive cancer,

00:03:54.211 --> 00:03:56.354
we were scheduling Jake's
first chemo appointment.

00:03:56.354 --> 00:03:59.896
Because of that, we had to
freeze some of Jake's soldiers

00:03:59.896 --> 00:04:01.992
in case they got affected by the chemo.

00:04:01.992 --> 00:04:03.396
Now, we were very much in love,

00:04:03.396 --> 00:04:05.858
but at six months we were
deciding who his sample

00:04:05.858 --> 00:04:08.025
would go to if something
were to happen to him.

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:10.928
Obviously we said me but it was a pretty

00:04:10.928 --> 00:04:13.074
intense conversation to have so early on.

00:04:13.074 --> 00:04:14.164
I'll say this.

00:04:14.164 --> 00:04:16.068
If you ever wanna know
if you've found the one,

00:04:16.068 --> 00:04:17.803
just have a near-death experience.

00:04:17.803 --> 00:04:19.235
Really clarifies things.

00:04:19.235 --> 00:04:21.545
Shortly after the second biopsy,

00:04:21.545 --> 00:04:23.673
Jake's neck started to expand,

00:04:23.673 --> 00:04:26.475
but in a new, different way.

00:04:26.475 --> 00:04:29.475
It was massive and hot to the touch.

00:04:30.395 --> 00:04:31.673
He then got a fever.

00:04:31.673 --> 00:04:34.668
It was probably around 100
at about 10 pm that night.

00:04:34.668 --> 00:04:37.606
So we called, and they
told us to hang tight

00:04:37.606 --> 00:04:38.821
until the next morning.

00:04:38.821 --> 00:04:40.063
That night did not go well.

00:04:40.063 --> 00:04:42.215
Jake's fever kept going
up through the night.

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:44.424
He was on a massive
amount of drugs so he kept

00:04:44.424 --> 00:04:45.359
passing out.

00:04:45.359 --> 00:04:47.470
Every time I was like,
"Let's go to the hospital,"

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.689
he'd pass out.

00:04:48.689 --> 00:04:49.737
And then I'd check his temperature,

00:04:49.737 --> 00:04:50.970
then I would pass out.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.961
And this went on until the morning.

00:04:52.961 --> 00:04:54.743
We're pretty sure he
almost died that night.

00:04:54.743 --> 00:04:57.254
We went to the hospital
the next day at seven am.

00:04:57.254 --> 00:04:59.224
By the time we were seen by the doctor,

00:04:59.224 --> 00:05:01.248
Jake literally had two heads.

00:05:01.248 --> 00:05:03.387
The doctor immediately opened Jake's neck

00:05:03.387 --> 00:05:05.999
and this next part is not
for the weak of stomach.

00:05:05.999 --> 00:05:09.255
Just an explosion of pus
came out of Jake's neck.

00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:10.688
I've never seen anything like it.

00:05:10.688 --> 00:05:13.669
It was like liquid vanilla pudding.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:16.313
In the midst of this, the
doctor asked if we were married.

00:05:16.313 --> 00:05:17.585
We said we were not.

00:05:17.585 --> 00:05:18.418
And the he told Jake,

00:05:18.418 --> 00:05:20.104
"You'd better propose after all of this."

00:05:20.104 --> 00:05:21.561
I actually think after you see the inside

00:05:21.561 --> 00:05:23.524
of someone's neck enough times,

00:05:23.524 --> 00:05:24.977
it's customary to get engaged.

00:05:24.977 --> 00:05:27.054
So we went back home
and Jake had a huge hole

00:05:27.054 --> 00:05:29.717
in his neck and couldn't lie
down 'cause it was draining

00:05:29.717 --> 00:05:31.997
and was just totally fucked
for a couple of weeks.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:34.477
Later that week, Jake had a full body MRI.

00:05:34.477 --> 00:05:37.059
They told us his body
was covered in masses.

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:38.364
We were devastated.

00:05:38.364 --> 00:05:41.459
We went home and waited for
the final biopsy results.

00:05:41.459 --> 00:05:43.768
They kept pushing Jake's
first chemo appointment back

00:05:43.768 --> 00:05:45.751
until they found the cell
they were looking for.

00:05:45.751 --> 00:05:48.153
This was three weeks of changing bandages,

00:05:48.153 --> 00:05:49.815
tons of medicine,

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:51.648
basically full time nursing.

00:05:51.648 --> 00:05:52.991
Sometime during the fourth week,

00:05:52.991 --> 00:05:54.295
we decided to go out to dinner to take

00:05:54.295 --> 00:05:55.877
our mind off of everything.

00:05:55.877 --> 00:05:58.136
Suddenly, Jake got a call from the doctor.

00:05:58.136 --> 00:06:01.017
He said they still hadn't found
anything in the biopsy yet

00:06:01.017 --> 00:06:04.471
which leads them to believe
it's a non Hodgkin's Lymphoma,

00:06:04.471 --> 00:06:08.456
which is far deadlier
than Hodgkin's Lymphoma.

00:06:08.456 --> 00:06:10.347
I cried into my enchiladas.

00:06:10.347 --> 00:06:11.970
Another week or so passed and they were

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.138
running the biopsy through a second scan.

00:06:14.138 --> 00:06:15.895
Eventually we were called in for a meeting

00:06:15.895 --> 00:06:18.282
with the oncologist with
Jake's first chemo session

00:06:18.282 --> 00:06:19.943
looming over our heads.

00:06:19.943 --> 00:06:23.496
Then he explained to us they
didn't find any cancer cells

00:06:23.496 --> 00:06:28.403
in the second scan, but they
did find Epstein-Barr, aka,

00:06:28.403 --> 00:06:29.236
mono.

00:06:30.971 --> 00:06:32.576
It was a huge relief.

00:06:32.576 --> 00:06:36.212
We had a new lease on life
and Jake had a giant scar

00:06:36.212 --> 00:06:37.426
on his neck forever.

00:06:37.426 --> 00:06:38.852
Now, I bet you're asking yourself,

00:06:38.852 --> 00:06:40.041
"Why didn't you sue?"

00:06:40.041 --> 00:06:42.759
Being there in the moment,
I had no doubt those doctors

00:06:42.759 --> 00:06:44.299
were trying to save Jake's life.

00:06:44.299 --> 00:06:46.496
They truly believed he was about to die.

00:06:46.496 --> 00:06:48.936
It may not have gone perfectly, but,

00:06:48.936 --> 00:06:50.580
they're human and shit happens.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.479
No obstacle seems to big after you've been

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:54.140
through something like that together.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.060
We were engaged shortly after.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.907
When you feel so close to
losing someone you love,

00:06:59.907 --> 00:07:01.625
you just don't wanna
waste any time getting

00:07:01.625 --> 00:07:03.125
your life started.

00:07:04.162 --> 00:07:07.495
(soft orchestral music)

